DESCRIPTION >
    Which of these event keys is already in the log.

    The sink calls this immediately before it appends a batch, and appends only
    the keys that come back missing. Two things fall out of that:

    - a retry of a batch that already landed writes nothing at all, so the
      physical duplicates the read-time collapse has to hide stay rare;
    - `write` can report `appended` and `duplicates` truthfully. Tinybird's
      ingestion endpoint reports rows accepted, and cannot report rows that
      turned out to be copies, because the collapse has not happened yet.

    It is a check-then-append, so it is racy by construction: two processes can
    both find a key missing and both append it. That is harmless here and it is
    worth being exact about why. The keys are derived from the events, the copies
    are therefore identical, and both the engine merge and every endpoint's
    `GROUP BY key` keep exactly one of them. The check is an optimisation and an
    honest counter, never the thing that enforces the guarantee.

    `date_from` and `date_to` bound the batch's own event times so the lookup
    prunes to the partitions the batch can be in. A key stored under an
    `occurred_at` outside that range is missed and appended a second time, which
    the collapse absorbs.

TOKEN "medusa_usage" READ

NODE endpoint
SQL >
    %
    SELECT DISTINCT key
    FROM usage_events
    WHERE
        key IN {{Array(keys, 'String', required=True, description='Event keys to look for.')}}
        AND occurred_at >= parseDateTime64BestEffort({{String(date_from, '1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z', required=True, description='Earliest event time in the batch, ISO 8601.')}}, 3, 'UTC')
        AND occurred_at <= parseDateTime64BestEffort({{String(date_to, '1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z', required=True, description='Latest event time in the batch, ISO 8601. Inclusive: this bounds a set of known instants, not a reporting period.')}}, 3, 'UTC')

TYPE ENDPOINT
